#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1440.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229986} :activity/id #uuid "b757aeb4-02cf-539c-8183-7dd7b2d63500" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049677 :topic/id #uuid "0a1b2bcd-7498-533c-be7a-464a282d3a31" :topic/math-academy-id 6701} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229986 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230203 :tutorial/content "So far, we've used differential equations to model quantities in circuits containing resistors and inductors. Now, we'll focus on circuits containing resistors and capacitors.\n\nA **capacitor** is a component of an electrical circuit that opposes *changes* in voltage across it by storing energy in an electric field.\n\n![](images/37/3729fc4786e8677a1a7bf20aa013d6c6943c6d69c73adc63dd743c3918e65db4.png)\n\nThe capacitance $C$ (measured in farads) of a capacitor determines how much charge $q$ (measured in coulombs) the capacitor stores for a given voltage, $V_C,$ according to the equation \n\n$$\nq = CV_C.\n$$\n\nAn **RC circuit** is an electrical circuit that contains both a resistor and a capacitor, most commonly in series with a voltage source. An example, with current $I(t),$ is shown below, consisting of a resistance $R,$ in ohms, a capacitance $C,$ in farads, and a source electromotive force (emf) $E,$ in volts.\n\n![](images/fe/feef8e5b715b6b0442dc27a65886d611b27d4d912842f992790e6309a78afda4.png)\n\nLet $q(t)$ denote the charge on the capacitor, measured in coulombs, as a function of time $t.$ Can we find a differential equation that best describes the charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor?\n\nFirst, note that since current is the rate at which electric charge flows, the circuit's current is the derivative of charge with respect to time: \n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nNow, recall Kirchhoff's Voltage law, which states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops around the loop.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The resistor *opposes the flow of current*, hence producing a voltage drop $V_R$ proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R{:}$\n\n$$\nV_R = RI = R\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\n- The capacitor *opposes changes in voltage*, hence producing a voltage drop $V_C$ related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ So, \n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's Voltage Law, the source voltage $E$ is the sum of the voltage drops: $E = V_R + V_C.$\n\nAs a result, the basic equation governing the amount of charge on the capacitor $q$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\nE = R\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{q}{C}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{1}{RC}q = \\dfrac{E}{R}.\n$$\n\nNote that since $I(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t},$ solving for $q(t)$ also tells us the current in the circuit." :tutorial/id #uuid "09f3c015-b628-5d46-bdb8-7bab430a5c0f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14571 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c65bf754-eef0-5e84-bd00-35163477c0bb" :step/math-academy-id 590193 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229987}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229987 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230204 :tutorial/content "Consider a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\sin(2t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistance of $40\\,\\Omega,$ and a capacitance of $0.0025\\,\\textrm{F}.$ Let's find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs:\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 40\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 40I = 40\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{0.0025} = 400q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n8\\sin(2t) = 40\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 400q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 10q = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sin(2t).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f5a25a61-fffc-5324-8a7e-46c6697292bf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14573 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "9e6b2bca-9335-5322-8c53-df90532ee3d4" :step/math-academy-id 590194 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229988}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229988 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229134 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228927 :question/id #uuid "353e21f2-2d1d-55e3-ac86-19932d117997" :question/math-academy-id "e-21759"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c51535d3-6b6a-5df3-a41f-9bad8e2edfbe" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051040}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229691} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498598}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit"} :step/id #uuid "10ceda14-e108-59d7-a5da-7c171a7f8f56" :step/math-academy-id 590195 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229989}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229989 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230206 :tutorial/content "In a first-order linear differential equation, the solution naturally splits into a transient term that decays over time and a steady-state term that remains as $t \\to \\infty$.\n\nIn an RC circuit with a constant electromotive force (EMF), this steady-state value corresponds to the charge the capacitor approaches as $t \\to \\infty,$ regardless of the initial charge. Physically, this represents the maximum charge the capacitor can store under a constant applied voltage.\n\nTo demonstrate how to identify the steady-state charge directly from the general solution of an RC circuit model, suppose the charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, in a series RC circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 12q = \\dfrac{7}{10}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} +12q_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nq_c(t) = Ae^{-12t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $q_p = \\alpha$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_p}{\\mathrm{d}t} = (\\alpha)' = 0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0 + 12\\alpha = \\dfrac{7}{10}\n  \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n  \\alpha = \\dfrac{7}{120}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is $q_p(t) = \\dfrac{7}{120}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-12t} + \\dfrac{7}{120}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c = Ae^{-12t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The constant $q_p = \\dfrac{7}{120}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to\\infty,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge (the charge the capacitor approaches as time increases) is ${\\dfrac{7}{120}}$ coulombs." :tutorial/id #uuid "ea3cd500-dfe2-5980-bdd5-a70d81a1b019" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14587 :tutorial/title "Steady-State Charges for Constant Voltage RC Circuits"} :step/id #uuid "40af9ba4-9866-5456-ae0c-24676110c40f" :step/math-academy-id 590198 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229990}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229990 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229135 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228928 :question/id #uuid "28fd19b3-762a-572e-9b52-27e17093ad93" :question/math-academy-id "e-21762"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b88fec8-167e-5900-9ae8-109affedf08c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049543}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498599}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit: Constant EMF"} :step/id #uuid "30306ff1-de14-5d7a-b50f-2c053569ee9f" :step/math-academy-id 590199 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229991}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229991 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230207 :tutorial/content "In a first-order linear RC circuit driven by a sinusoidal voltage, the solution consists of a transient charge, which decays over time, and a steady-state charge, which oscillates with the same frequency as the input.\n\nAs time increases, the transient charge vanishes, leaving a steady-state charge that oscillates periodically and is independent of the initial condition.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the amplitude of the steady-state charge in the RC circuit below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc700238d93fd6529c7113cd1a650e8c03d796e606afd4b655123cd04ed11cc7.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\cos(4t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor with resistance $50\\,\\Omega,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $\\dfrac{1}{150}\\,\\textrm{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs:\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\textrm d q}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 50\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 50I = 50\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{150}\\right)} = 150q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n20\\cos(4t) = 50\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 150q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 3q = \\dfrac{2}{5}\\cos(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 3q_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nq_c(t) = Ae^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(4t) + \\beta\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+3(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +3\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ 3\\alpha +4\\beta =\\frac{2}{5}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = \\dfrac{6}{125}$ and $\\beta = \\dfrac{8}{125}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\dfrac{6}{125}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{8}{125}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-3t} + \\dfrac{6}{125}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{8}{125}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c(t) = Ae^{-3t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $q_p(t) = \\dfrac{6}{125}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{8}{125}\\sin(4t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t) \\approx q_p(t) = \\dfrac{6}{125}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{8}{125}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{6}{125}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{8}{125}\\right)^2} =0.08\\,\\textrm{coulombs}.\n$$\n\nThis amplitude represents the maximum magnitude of the oscillating steady-state charge on the capacitor." :tutorial/id #uuid "5d9cda5d-0b3c-5320-a70a-71d53eedf1aa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14588 :tutorial/title "Steady-State Charges for Sinusoidal Voltage RC Circuits"} :step/id #uuid "d11623bd-24d5-5e52-9186-276626749839" :step/math-academy-id 590203 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229992}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229992 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229136 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228929 :question/id #uuid "a3e7a060-f36d-5fd6-a6f6-5b321261fb0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21763"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96b32f48-aea6-5ccd-94e9-47141767a803" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050392}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498556}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"} :step/id #uuid "00e01a01-a199-5457-88d2-64f8282b622a" :step/math-academy-id 590204}] :activity/title "Modeling RC Circuits With First-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186229262}]}
